WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.040
In January 2010, inspectors with the UN’s
nuclear monitoring agency noticed something

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.650
strange at the nuclear facility in Natanz,
Iran.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:14.030
Numerous centrifuges at the crown jewel of
Iran’s nuclear program were failing.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.970
Centrifuges are devices that produce enriched
uranium which can be used to make nuclear

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.970
weapons.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.840
The cause of their failure was a mystery.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.960
It’s believed the U.S. and Israel ordered
a cyberattack to slow down Iran’s nuclear

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:26.960
program.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.330
A computer worm called Stuxnet traveled on
USB thumb drives and spread through Microsoft

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:37.250
Windows computers that controlled the centrifuges,
sending instructions for them to burn out.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.410
Stuxnet didn’t simply take over a computer
or steal information, it actually caused the

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.720
equipment to self-destruct!

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.010
It’s more dangerous than a computer virus.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:50.610
While a virus is dormant until the unwitting
victim installs it, a worm can spread without

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.080
being activated.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.360
Stuxnet is believed to have been created by
the U.S. National Security Agency and Israeli

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.900
intelligence though neither has ever admitted
responsibility.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.860
Their collaborative effort was dubbed “Operation
Olympic Games”

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.920
It started under the Bush administration.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.540
President Obama sped it up when he came into
office in 2009, heeding Bush’s advice to

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.500
continue the cyberattacks.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.560
President Bush believed it was the only way
to prevent Israel from launching a conventional

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.170
strike on Iranian nuclear facilities - like
the one that targeted a Syrian nuclear reactor

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.970
in 2007.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.670
Israel has never bought Iran’s argument
that its nuclear program is entirely peaceful,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.630
for the purposes of generating electricity,
for example.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.670
Iran launched its nuclear program in the 1950s
with the help of the U.S. through the Atoms

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:38.640
for Peace program in which America shared
resources with countries wanting to develop

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.190
atomic energy for peaceful activities.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:46.170
In 1970, Iran ratified the Non-Proliferation
Treaty (NPT) to limit its program for peaceful

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.170
use.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.450
It meant Iran would have to be subject to
inspection by the International Atomic Energy

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.760
Agency (IAEA).

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.820
Those inspectors with the IAEA noticed centrifuges
mysteriously failing at the start of 2010.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.770
Getting the worm into the Natanz facility
was actually the final step of a carefully

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.780
planned attack.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.690
This is what is believed to have happened.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.359
The NSA and the Israeli military wrote a program
that digitally mapped out the workings of

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:12.029
Iran’s Natanz plant.

00:02:12.029 --> 00:02:16.260
The program is introduced into a controller
computer at the plant possibly by an oblivious

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.480
plant worker.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.810
The program collects information on how the
computers in the facility are configured

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.180
The agencies use the data to design a complex
worm - Stuxnet.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.700
Stuxnet is introduced into the plant’s computer
controllers via a USB stick.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.129
Stuxnet was designed to target computers with
a specific Siemens (see-mins) software program.

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:39.640
This program was used by industrial computers
to control and monitor the centrifuges.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.250
The worm takes over control of the centrifuges,
causing them to spin too fast or too slow.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.220
They become unbalanced and in some cases,
explode.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.870
Centrifuges spin at supersonic speeds to separate
isotopes in uranium gas.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.169
Uranium enriched to 90% or more can be used
to make nuclear weapons.

00:02:57.169 --> 00:03:01.370
According to the New York Times, the centrifuges
first began to crash in 2008.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.469
Initially, the attacks were small and seemed
like random accidents as variations in the

00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:09.769
code prompted different types of breakdowns
which confused the Iranian engineers.

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:15.250
By the spring of 2010, the NSA and Israel’s
Secretive Intelligence Unit 8200 prepared

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.099
to ramp up their attack to target 1,000 centrifuges.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:19.950
But the worm was discovered.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.989
That summer, a programming error sent the
worm onto the laptop of an Iranian engineer.

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:29.120
The laptop had reportedly been hooked up to
the systems controlling the centrifuges.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.099
The Stuxnet worm was replicated across the
internet, spreading wildly and attacking computers

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:35.829
in India, Indonesia, and China.

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:40.110
It’s eventually tracked down and disassembled
by security researchers.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:45.989
President Obama decided to continue the operation
anyway, and Stuxnet took out nearly 1,000

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:48.329
centrifuges - or about a fifth of those operating.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:52.769
There’s been debate about how the worm actually
got into the nuclear facility.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:57.680
According to one theory, Israel hired an Iranian
double agent or agents to infect the facility

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.430
with a corrupt memory stick.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.620
Another theory, according to security software
firm Symantec is that the attackers infected

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.629
computers at five outside companies connected
to the nuclear program, and somehow, managed

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:10.959
to find its way to Natanz.

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:15.279
Experts who analyzed the computer worm say
it was far more complex - and brilliant - than

00:04:15.279 --> 00:04:17.269
anything they had ever seen.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:21.139
The attackers even secretly recorded what
a normal day at the plant looked like and

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:25.039
then played those readings back to plant operators
so they didn’t think anything was wrong

00:04:25.039 --> 00:04:27.121
as the centrifuges were tearing themselves
apart.

00:04:27.121 --> 00:04:32.180
Kind of like a pre-recorded security tape
during a bank heist in movies, like Ocean’s

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.180
11.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.539
The U.S. believes the attack set back Iran’s
nuclear program by up to 2 years.

00:04:37.539 --> 00:04:39.710
Others believe this is an overestimation.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:44.380
Iran denied that its well-known struggles
to enrich uranium were caused by Stuxnet.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.760
The country had always insisted its nuclear
program was for peaceful purposes.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:54.259
That is until a former head of Iran’s nuclear
agency let slip in 2021 that Iran’s growth

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:58.210
involved “satellites, missiles, and nuclear
weapons".

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.690
Fereydoun Abbas-Davani spoke to state media
on the anniversary of the assassination of

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:07.050
Iranian nuclear scientist Mohsen Fakhrizadeh
by the Israeli government.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.319
Currently, the Biden administration is trying
to revive a 2015 agreement that curbs Iran’s

00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:15.000
nuclear work in return for relief from sanctions.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.629
It would see Iranian oil come back on the
market at a time when the U.S. has banned

00:05:18.629 --> 00:05:19.770
Russian oil.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.349
President Trump had exited the nuclear deal.

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:26.780
Critics worry that Iran could develop nukes
under the guise of working toward peaceful

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.070
ends.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.860
The Stuxnet computer worm has vast repercussions
today.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.189
Computer security experts have described Stuxnet
as an instruction manual of sorts.

00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:40.870
Sean McGurk, a former cybersecurity official
at the U.S. Department of Homeland Security,

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.569
noted that the Stuxnet source code could be
downloaded, modified, and directed at new

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:46.569
targets.

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:49.900
During an interview with 60 Minutes, he said
this in reference to those who created the

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.590
sophisticated cyberweapon:

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.860
"They opened the box.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.750
They demonstrated the capability...it's not
something that can be put back."

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:01.940
In 2012, Iranian hackers are believed to have
unleashed a virus against the Saudi Arabian

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.510
petroleum and natural gas company Saudi Aramco.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:09.560
They stole passwords, destroyed data, and
prevented rebooting on tens of thousands of

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:10.560
computers.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.009
A document leaked by Edward Snowden and provided
to The Intercept showed the NSA believed Iran

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:19.840
had “demonstrated a clear ability to learn
from the capabilities and actions of others.”

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.860
Strengthening the fears of the U.S. intelligence
community that Iran had learned from American

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.099
cyberattacks.

